WEBVTT

00:00.590 --> 00:02.820
Here I've drawn the
most classic parabola,

00:02.820 --> 00:05.040
y is equal to x squared.

00:05.040 --> 00:09.710
And what I want to do is think
about what happens-- or how can

00:09.710 --> 00:12.660
I go about shifting
this parabola.

00:12.660 --> 00:14.860
And so let's think about
a couple of examples.

00:14.860 --> 00:16.940
So let's think about
the graph of the curve.

00:16.940 --> 00:19.790
This is y is equal to x squared.

00:19.790 --> 00:23.160
Let's think about what
the curve of y minus k

00:23.160 --> 00:24.550
is equal to x squared.

00:24.550 --> 00:26.770
What would this look like?

00:26.770 --> 00:29.330
Well, right over here, we
see when x is equal to 0,

00:29.330 --> 00:31.380
x squared is equal to 0.

00:31.380 --> 00:33.120
That's this yellow curve.

00:33.120 --> 00:36.470
So x squared is equal to y,
or y is equal to x squared.

00:36.470 --> 00:38.940
But for this one, x
squared isn't equal to y.

00:38.940 --> 00:41.270
It's equal to y minus k.

00:41.270 --> 00:44.090
So when x equals a
0, and we square it,

00:44.090 --> 00:45.770
0 squared doesn't get us to y.

00:45.770 --> 00:47.930
It gets us to y minus k.

00:47.930 --> 00:50.860
So this is going to
be k less than y.

00:50.860 --> 00:52.870
Or another way of thinking
about it, this is 0.

00:52.870 --> 00:57.590
If it's k less than y, y must
be at k, wherever k might be.

00:57.590 --> 01:00.460
So y must be at k,
right over there.

01:00.460 --> 01:02.640
So at least for this
point, it had the effect

01:02.640 --> 01:07.120
of shifting up the y value by k.

01:07.120 --> 01:09.670
And that's actually true
for any of these values.

01:09.670 --> 01:12.690
So let's think about x
being right over here.

01:12.690 --> 01:15.796
For this yellow curve,
you square this x value,

01:15.796 --> 01:16.670
and you get it there.

01:16.670 --> 01:18.490
And it's clearly not
drawn to scale the way

01:18.490 --> 01:19.906
that I've done it
right over here.

01:22.170 --> 01:23.930
But now for this
curve right over here,

01:23.930 --> 01:25.085
x squared doesn't cut it.

01:25.085 --> 01:26.950
It only gets you to y minus k.

01:26.950 --> 01:31.340
So y must be k higher than this.

01:31.340 --> 01:34.850
So this is y minus k. y
must be k higher than this.

01:34.850 --> 01:37.710
So y must be right over here.

01:37.710 --> 01:41.540
So this curve is essentially
this blue curve shifted up

01:41.540 --> 01:44.410
by k.

01:44.410 --> 01:47.950
So making it y minus k is equal
to x squared shifted it up

01:47.950 --> 01:48.770
by k.

01:48.770 --> 01:52.450
Whatever value this
is, shift it up by k.

01:52.450 --> 01:56.230
This distance is a constant
k, the vertical distance

01:56.230 --> 01:59.090
between these two parabolas.

01:59.090 --> 02:02.450
And I'll try to draw
it as cleanly as I can.

02:02.450 --> 02:05.370
This vertical distance
is a constant k.

02:05.370 --> 02:08.270
Now let's think about shifting
in the horizontal direction.

02:08.270 --> 02:12.710
Let's think about what happens
if I were to say y is equal to,

02:12.710 --> 02:19.070
not x squared, but
x minus h squared.

02:19.070 --> 02:20.070
So let's think about it.

02:20.070 --> 02:24.230
This is the value you would get
for y when you just square 0.

02:24.230 --> 02:26.160
You get y is equal to 0.

02:26.160 --> 02:28.830
How do we get y
equals 0 over here?

02:28.830 --> 02:32.470
Well, this quantity right
over here has to be 0.

02:32.470 --> 02:36.550
So x minus h has to be 0,
or x has to be equal to h.

02:36.550 --> 02:38.280
So let's say that h
is right over here.

02:38.280 --> 02:40.940
So x has to be equal to h.

02:40.940 --> 02:43.990
So one way to think about
it is, whatever value you

02:43.990 --> 02:46.890
were squaring here
to get your y,

02:46.890 --> 02:49.590
you now have to have
an h higher value

02:49.590 --> 02:50.810
to square that same thing.

02:50.810 --> 02:52.601
Because you're going
to subtract h from it.

02:52.601 --> 02:58.320
Just to get to 0,
x has to equal h.

02:58.320 --> 03:02.070
Here, if you wanted to square
1, x just had to be equal to 1.

03:02.070 --> 03:04.280
So here, let's just say,
for the sake of argument,

03:04.280 --> 03:07.080
that this is x is equal to 1.

03:07.080 --> 03:10.310
And this is 1 squared,
clearly not drawn to scale.

03:10.310 --> 03:12.170
So that would be 1, as well.

03:12.170 --> 03:15.590
But now to square 1, we don't
have to just get x equals 1.

03:15.590 --> 03:17.260
x has to be h plus 1.

03:17.260 --> 03:18.970
It has to be 1 higher than h.

03:18.970 --> 03:22.700
It has to be h plus 1 to
get to that same point.

03:22.700 --> 03:25.390
So you see the net
effect is that instead

03:25.390 --> 03:28.470
of squaring just x,
but squaring x minus h,

03:28.470 --> 03:31.480
we shifted the
curve to the right.

03:31.480 --> 03:36.330
So the curve-- let me do this in
this purple color, this magenta

03:36.330 --> 03:38.070
color-- will look like this.

03:38.070 --> 03:41.270
We shifted it to the right.

03:41.270 --> 03:48.620
And we shifted it
to the right by h.

03:48.620 --> 03:51.040
Now let's think of another
thought experiment.

03:51.040 --> 03:56.120
Let's imagine that-- let's
think about the curve

03:56.120 --> 04:01.390
y is equal to
negative x squared.

04:01.390 --> 04:03.415
Well, now whatever the
value of x squared is,

04:03.415 --> 04:05.040
we're going to take
the negative of it.

04:05.040 --> 04:07.250
So here, no matter what
x we took, we squared it.

04:07.250 --> 04:08.640
We get a positive value.

04:08.640 --> 04:10.670
Now we're always going
to get a negative value

04:10.670 --> 04:12.687
once we multiply it
times a negative 1.

04:12.687 --> 04:14.020
So it's going to look like this.

04:14.020 --> 04:15.530
It's going to be a
mirror image of y

04:15.530 --> 04:20.010
equals x squared reflected
over the horizontal axis.

04:20.010 --> 04:22.040
So it's going to look
something like that.

04:22.040 --> 04:26.480
So that's y is equal
to negative x squared.

04:26.480 --> 04:28.890
And now let's just imagine
scaling it even more.

04:28.890 --> 04:38.520
What would y equal
negative 2x squared?

04:38.520 --> 04:40.340
Well, actually, let
me do two things.

04:40.340 --> 04:43.220
So what would y equals
2x squared look like?

04:43.220 --> 04:45.060
So let's just take
the positive version,

04:45.060 --> 04:47.671
so y equals 2x squared.

04:47.671 --> 04:49.170
Well, now as we
square things, we're

04:49.170 --> 04:50.336
going to multiply them by 2.

04:50.336 --> 04:51.730
So it's going to
increase faster.

04:51.730 --> 04:53.480
So it's going to look
something like this.

04:53.480 --> 04:56.269
It's going to be
narrower and steeper.

04:56.269 --> 04:57.810
So it might look
something like this.

04:57.810 --> 04:59.880
And once again, I'm just
giving you the idea.

04:59.880 --> 05:01.890
I haven't really
drawn this to scale.

05:01.890 --> 05:06.110
So increasing it by a factor
will make it increase faster.

05:06.110 --> 05:12.000
If we did y equals
negative 2x squared,

05:12.000 --> 05:14.501
well, then it's going to get
negative faster on either side.

05:14.501 --> 05:16.250
So it's going to look
something like this.

05:16.250 --> 05:18.570
It's going to be the mirror
image of what I just drew.

05:18.570 --> 05:22.870
So it's going to be a narrower
parabola just like that.

05:22.870 --> 05:25.670
And similarly-- and I know that
my diagram is getting really

05:25.670 --> 05:27.810
messy right now--
but just remember

05:27.810 --> 05:29.580
we started with y
equals x squared,

05:29.580 --> 05:31.830
which is this curve
right over here.

05:31.830 --> 05:35.880
What happens if we did
y equals 1/2 x squared?

05:35.880 --> 05:37.760
I'm running out of
colors, as well.

05:37.760 --> 05:40.180
If we did y equals
1/2 x squared,

05:40.180 --> 05:42.180
well, then the thing's
going to increase slower.

05:42.180 --> 05:45.220
It's going to look the same,
but it's going to open up wider.

05:45.220 --> 05:46.580
It's going to increase slower.

05:46.580 --> 05:50.160
It's going to look
something like this.

05:50.160 --> 05:52.270
So this hopefully
gives you a sense

05:52.270 --> 05:55.360
of how we can shift
parabolas around.

05:55.360 --> 06:04.190
So for example, if I have-- and
I'm doing a very rough drawing

06:04.190 --> 06:05.940
here to give you the
general idea of what

06:05.940 --> 06:08.121
we're talking about.

06:08.121 --> 06:13.050
So if this is y
equals x squared,

06:13.050 --> 06:15.360
so that's the graph
of y equals x squared.

06:22.500 --> 06:25.240
Let me do this in a color
that I haven't used yet--

06:25.240 --> 06:34.991
the graph of y minus k is equal
to A times x minus h squared

06:34.991 --> 06:36.240
will look something like this.

06:36.240 --> 06:39.440
Instead of the vertex
being at 0, 0, the vertex--

06:39.440 --> 06:40.899
or the lowest, or
I guess you could

06:40.899 --> 06:42.440
say the minimum or
the maximum point,

06:42.440 --> 06:44.570
the extreme point in the
parabola, this point right

06:44.570 --> 06:47.069
over here, would be the maximum
point for a downward opening

06:47.069 --> 06:49.856
parabola, a minimum point for
an upward opening parabola--

06:49.856 --> 06:50.980
that's going to be shifted.

06:50.980 --> 06:56.630
It's going to be shifted
by h to the right and k up.

06:56.630 --> 06:58.740
So its vertex is going
to be right over here.

06:58.740 --> 07:03.674
And it's going to be scaled
by A. So if A is equal to 1,

07:03.674 --> 07:04.840
it's going to look the same.

07:04.840 --> 07:07.770
It's going to have
the same opening.

07:07.770 --> 07:09.310
So that's A equals 1.

07:09.310 --> 07:14.440
If A is greater than 1, it's
going to be steeper, like this.

07:14.440 --> 07:17.530
If A is less than 1
but greater than 0,

07:17.530 --> 07:20.240
it's just going to be
wider opening, like that.

07:23.370 --> 07:28.190
Actually, if A is 0, then it
just turns into a flat line.

07:28.190 --> 07:31.410
And then if A is negative
but less than negative 1,

07:31.410 --> 07:34.172
it's kind of a broad-opening
thing like that.

07:34.172 --> 07:36.100
Or I should say greater
than negative 1.

07:36.100 --> 07:37.580
If it's between
0 and negative 1,

07:37.580 --> 07:39.600
it will be a broad-opening
thing like that.

07:39.600 --> 07:41.580
At negative 1, it'll
look like a reflection

07:41.580 --> 07:44.110
of our original curve.

07:44.110 --> 07:49.287
And then if A is less
than negative 1--

07:49.287 --> 07:50.870
so it's even more
negative-- then it's

07:50.870 --> 07:54.130
going to be even a
steeper parabola that

07:54.130 --> 07:55.497
might look like that.

07:55.497 --> 07:57.080
So hopefully that
gives you a good way

07:57.080 --> 08:00.640
of how to shift and
scale parabolas.
